# Numbers.
#
# `floor`, `round` and `decimalOf` are primitives because they are
# statements about the f64 representation §14A.3 chose, which the language
# gives no way to observe. Formatting a number as text is not here at all:
# it is `text of n`, whose emission is the platform's own shortest-round-trip
# printer, because reimplementing that in ZDeceptron would produce a second
# answer to a question that already has one.
#
# `min`, `max`, `abs` and `clamp` are polymorphic over `Whole` and
# `Decimal`. They are the reason a variable carrying a built-in operand
# constraint has to be generalisable (§17.4.4): pinned to whichever of the
# two happened to be used first, three of these four would be unusable on
# the other.
#
# ⚠️ DIVISION. `/` gives a `Decimal` whatever it divides — see the note on
# `BinOp::Div` in `zdc-types/src/infer.rs` for why it is not allowed to
# mean two operations. `quotient` and `mod` below are integer division and
# its remainder, and they are *floored*, not truncated: `quotient` is
# defined as `floor of (value / divisor)`, so the language has one integer
# division rather than two that disagree below zero. That also makes
# `mod` non-negative for a positive divisor, which is what a wraparound
# wants — `mod with value is 0 - 1, divisor is 8` is `Some with value is 7`,
# and a torus works.
#
# ⚠️ WHAT A `Whole` IS, AND WHY THE NARROWING GIVES AN `Option`. §14A.3
# rules that a `Whole` is a **finite** integral f64 and that a `Decimal`
# is **every** f64 — `Infinity`, `-Infinity` and `NaN` included, because
# IEEE-754 division produces all three and `Decimal` is the type that
# names IEEE-754. `floor of` and `round of` are the only route from the
# second set into the first, so they are the only place the narrowing can
# fail, and §5.4 already settled what a partial operation gives: an
# `Option`, exactly as `at` does. `floor of (1 / 0)` is `None`. It is no
# longer an `Infinity` held in a value whose type says `Whole`, which
# §5.4's own list of TypeScript unsoundnesses calls "type erasure lying
# about runtime values".
#
# This is the type system catching up with a ruling the storage layer had
# already made on its own: `zdc-store`'s `Number` refuses a non-finite on
# parse, and its `plus` gives `None` on overflow rather than writing
# `Infinity` into a key that `JSON.parse` would then reject for ever.
# A `durable Whole` was never able to hold one.
#
# ⚠️ THE BITWISE WINDOW. The six bitwise primitives read the low 32 bits of
# their operands and give an **unsigned** 32-bit `Whole`, in `0 … 4294967295`.
# One window and one signedness, so there is no `>>` and `>>>` pair to
# choose between. A shift count is taken modulo 32, so `places is 32` is
# `places is 0` and a negative count is a large one — the platform's rule,
# stated here because a program that shifts by a computed amount will
# meet it. They are primitives for the same reason `floor` is: they
# are statements about the f64 representation §14A.3 chose, which the
# language gives no way to observe. A ZDeceptron definition would have to
# take a number apart bit by bit through `mod`, at thirty-two frames per
# operation and with no way to reproduce 32-bit wraparound — which is not
# an inconvenience but the fact that the language has no integer type
# narrower than f64.
#
# ⚠️ RANDOMNESS IS NOT A PRIMITIVE, AND DELIBERATELY SO. `nextSeed` and
# `randomBits` below are mulberry32, written in ordinary ZDeceptron over
# those six. A generator is therefore a **pure function of a seed the
# program owns**, and the language acquires no source of entropy: there is
# nothing here that could make a `static` value differ between two builds,
# so §17.4.7's argument against a random seed and the build evaluator's
# determinism (§17.4.8 — a build that fails, fails everywhere) are both
# untouched. Where the seed comes from is the program's decision and it is
# already spellable: a literal for a reproducible run, or `clock` in a
# signal initialiser, which is exactly where `time.zd` says a freshness
# decision is written down.
#
# Every operation in this file is O(1).

foreign floor is anywhere
    from "zd:number" as "floor"
    takes of value is Decimal
    gives pure Option of Whole

foreign round is anywhere
    from "zd:number" as "round"
    takes of value is Decimal
    gives pure Option of Whole

foreign decimalOf is anywhere
    from "zd:number" as "decimalOf"
    takes of value is Whole
    gives pure Decimal

function min with first, second
    if first < second
        give first
    give second

function max with first, second
    if first > second
        give first
    give second

function abs of value
    if value < 0
        give 0 - value
    give value

function clamp with value, low, high
    give min with first is (max with first is value, second is low), second is high

# Integer division, floored. O(1).
#
# `floor of (value / divisor)` and nothing else, so that the two spellings
# cannot give different answers.
#
# ⚠️ A ZERO DIVISOR IS `None`, NOT `Infinity`. `quotient with value is 1,
# divisor is 0` used to give `Infinity` and `mod` with the same used to
# give `NaN`, both held in a value whose type said `Whole`. That was never
# a property of these two functions — `floor of` and `round of` declared
# `gives Whole` and returned `Infinity` for an infinite `Decimal`, so the
# hole was theirs and predated the numeric library — and it is closed at
# its source rather than here: §14A.3 now says a `Whole` is finite, and
# the narrowing that can fail says so in its type. These two inherit the
# `Option` and add nothing to it. Eliminate it with `valueOr`, exactly as
# for `at`. `zdc-codegen/tests/library.rs` recorded the old answers as a
# defect and now asserts the new ones.
function quotient with value, divisor
    give floor of (value / divisor)

# The remainder that goes with `quotient`, so that
# `divisor * (quotient …) + (mod …)` is `value` exactly. O(1).
#
# Non-negative whenever `divisor` is positive, which is the property a
# wraparound index needs and the one JavaScript's own `%` does not have.
#
# `None` in exactly the cases `quotient` is `None`, so the two still
# describe one division rather than two functions that disagree at zero.
function mod with value, divisor
    when quotient with value is value, divisor is divisor
        Some with whole
            give Some with value is (value - (divisor * whole))
        None
            give None

# --- reading a number out of text ---------------------------------------
#
# `text of n` is the way out of a number and this is the way in, which is
# the direction §5.2's `Input` needs: an `Input` binds a `Text`, so
# without these an age, a quantity, a price or a score has no route from
# the box the user typed it in to the type the program computes with.
#
# ⚠️ WHAT COUNTS AS A NUMBER. `parseDecimal` accepts an optional sign,
# digits with an optional fractional part, and an optional exponent, with
# any amount of surrounding whitespace. That is the language's own numeric
# literal plus a sign, and it is deliberately narrower than JavaScript's
# `Number`, which the platform would otherwise decide this for. Four of
# `Number`'s answers are refused here by name:
#
#   ""          `Number("")` is 0. An empty field is not a zero, and a
#               form that reads it as one has invented a value nobody
#               typed. It is `None`.
#   "12abc"     `parseFloat` gives 12. A number with rubbish after it is
#               not a number, and taking the prefix is how a typo becomes
#               a quantity. It is `None`.
#   "0x1f"      `Number` gives 31. Neither the language nor a form field
#               has hexadecimal in it.
#   "Infinity"  `Number` gives an infinity, and so does `"1e400"` by
#               overflowing. §14A.3 makes `Decimal` every f64, so an
#               infinity is a legal `Decimal` and this is a choice rather
#               than a consequence: text that arrived from outside the
#               program is not an IEEE operation, so there is no division
#               here whose answer `Infinity` would be describing. `1 / 0`
#               is still how a program writes one.
#
# It is a primitive for the same reason `floor` is: a parse has to read
# the digits of a `Text` and weigh them into the f64 §14A.3 chose, and the
# language gives no way to observe either. `textAt` hands back a one
# character `Text`, not the number that character is, so a ZDeceptron
# definition could not even begin — and a definition that got there by
# comparing against ten literals would be a second, differently rounded
# answer to a question the platform's own parser already answers.
foreign parseDecimal is anywhere
    from "zd:number" as "parseDecimal"
    takes of value is Text
    gives pure Option of Decimal

# The same text read as a `Whole`, and **exactly**: a fractional input has
# no whole number and says so. O(1).
#
# Written in ZDeceptron rather than declared, which is the point: it is
# `parseDecimal`, then the narrowing `floor of` already provides, then a
# check that the narrowing lost nothing. So there is one parser in the
# library and not two that could disagree about `"0x1f"`.
#
# ⚠️ `"12.5"` IS `None`, NOT 12 AND NOT 13. Truncating would make a
# quantity field read a mistyped price as a smaller price, and rounding
# would make it read a larger one; either is a number the caller did not
# ask for, and §5.4's rule for a partial operation is to say so. A caller
# that wants truncation writes it — `floor of (decimalOr with value is
# typed, fallback is 0)` — and it is one line and visible at the call
# site.
#
# `"1e400"` is `None` here too, and for a second reason on top of
# `parseDecimal`'s: §14A.3 rules a `Whole` finite, so `floor of` would
# refuse it anyway.
function parseWhole of value
    when parseDecimal of value
        Some with number
            give exactWhole of number
        None
            give None

# The `Whole` that is exactly `number`, or `None` when narrowing would
# lose something. O(1).
#
# `floor of` and then back through `decimalOf`: a number survives the
# round trip exactly when it was already integral, and the comparison is
# the one place that fact is written down.
function exactWhole of number
    when floor of number
        Some with whole
            if (decimalOf of whole) is number
                give Some with value is whole
            give None
        None
            give None

# The fallback forms, shaped like `atOr`, `valueOr` and `readyOr`, so a
# numeric field is one expression rather than a `when`. O(1).
#
# The `Option` above is the honest answer and these are the elimination of
# it, exactly as `atOr` is `mapAt`'s. The fallback is the caller's: a
# library that chose 0 for an unparseable field would be choosing a
# quantity on the program's behalf.
function wholeOr with value, fallback
    give valueOr with maybe is (parseWhole of value), fallback is fallback

function decimalOr with value, fallback
    give valueOr with maybe is (parseDecimal of value), fallback is fallback

# --- a root and an exponent ---------------------------------------------
#
# Any geometry, any distance, any compound growth, and the Brier score a
# forecasting application is built on. Both are primitives, and for two
# different reasons.
#
# `sqrt` is the `text of` argument in another place: the platform's square
# root is correctly rounded, and a ZDeceptron definition by Newton's
# method would be a second answer to a question that already has one,
# differing in the last bit and therefore differing between a value
# computed here and the same value computed by anything else that touches
# the data.
#
# `power` cannot be written at all for a fractional exponent. Repeated
# multiplication reaches `power with value is 2, exponent is 10` and says
# nothing about `exponent is 0.5`, which is a root, and a root needs the
# exponential and the logarithm the language does not have.
#
# ⚠️ BOTH GIVE AN `Option`, AND THE RULE IS ONE LINE: **`None` unless the
# answer is a finite number.** That covers `sqrt of (0 - 1)`, which is
# `NaN`; `sqrt of (1 / 0)`, which is an infinity; `power with value is 10,
# exponent is 400`, which overflows; and `power with value is 0, exponent
# is 0 - 1`, which is division by zero wearing another name and which
# `quotient` already refuses.
#
# It is the same ruling `floor of` carries and it is deliberate rather
# than inherited: §14A.3 makes a `Decimal` every f64, so an infinity is a
# legal `Decimal` and these two *could* hand one back. Issue #5 records
# that `Whole` arithmetic on the client path is still unguarded, so there
# are ways to make an infinity that this file does not control. That is a
# reason not to add another, not a licence to. Eliminate it with
# `valueOr`, exactly as for `at`.
foreign sqrt is anywhere
    from "zd:number" as "sqrt"
    takes of value is Decimal
    gives pure Option of Decimal

foreign power is anywhere
    from "zd:number" as "power"
    takes value is Decimal, exponent is Decimal
    gives pure Option of Decimal

# --- formatting a number for display ------------------------------------
#
# ⚠️ THE PRELUDE DOES NOT DECLARE AN `Intl` FOREIGN, AND HERE IS WHY.
# The spec's position was that formatting is a user-level `foreign`
# against `Intl`, which also answers time zones, and the open question was
# whether the prelude declares that foreign once or every program declares
# its own. The answer is neither, and the reason is a fact about this
# library rather than a preference.
#
# Every prelude primitive is declared `is anywhere`, and that word is a
# claim that the operation can be linked into **every** root. There are
# three: the client bundle, §8.2's server root, and §17.4.8's build root,
# which runs inside the compiler's own sandbox. `Intl` is a browser and
# Node facility, and the engine the compiler embeds does not have it —
# `typeof Intl` there is `undefined`. So `foreign … is anywhere` over
# `Intl` would be false at one of the three, and
# `state price is static Text from money of amount` would fail in a build
# while the identical expression worked in a browser. A prelude operation
# whose meaning depends on the placement is precisely what the placement
# system exists to make impossible.
#
# There is a second reason under that one. An `Intl` result is a function
# of the *host's* CLDR data, so the same locale tag formats differently
# across browsers and across ICU versions. §17.4.8 requires that a build
# which fails, fails everywhere; a `static` number formatted through
# `Intl` would differ between two build machines. Passing the locale as
# an argument does not repair that, because the data behind the tag is
# still the host's.
#
# So locale-dependent formatting stays a user-level `foreign`, exactly
# where the spec put it, and now for a reason a test can check. What the
# prelude ships is the part that is the same at every placement, and it
# decomposes into three things of which **only one is a primitive**:
#
#   precision   `fixedText` below. A primitive, because `text of` is the
#               platform's shortest round-tripping printer and offers no
#               control at all, and because a fixed-point printer written
#               in ZDeceptron would have to read the digits of an f64,
#               which §14A.3 says the language cannot do.
#   grouping    a fold over the digits, written in ZDeceptron.
#   currency    a symbol and the side it goes on, written in ZDeceptron.
#
# Every function here is O(d²) in d, the number of digits displayed,
# because building a `Text` the source did not write means `+`. A
# displayed number has at most twenty-one digits before the point, so that
# is a constant with a small bound rather than a cost.

# `value` with exactly `digits` digits after the point, or `None`. O(1).
#
# ⚠️ THE ANSWER IS A SIGN, DIGITS AND AT MOST ONE POINT, and nothing else.
# `groupedText` below relies on that, so the three ways `toFixed` breaks
# it are `None` here rather than left to be discovered downstream:
# a count outside `0 … 100` throws a `RangeError`; a non-finite value
# renders as the text `"Infinity"` or `"NaN"`; and at or above 1e21 the
# platform switches to exponential notation.
#
# The rounding is the f64's, not the decimal one a reader may expect:
# `fixedText with value is 1.005, digits is 2` is `"1.00"`, because the
# nearest double to 1.005 is below it. That is §14A.3's choice showing
# through, and restating it here is cheaper than a program discovering it
# in a total.
foreign fixedText is anywhere
    from "zd:number" as "fixed"
    takes value is Decimal, digits is Whole
    gives pure Option of Text

# `value` at `digits` precision with `separator` between each group of
# three digits before the point. O(d²).
#
# The `Option` is `fixedText`'s and is not decorated: a number with no
# fixed-point form has no grouped form either.
function numberText with value, digits, separator
    when fixedText with value is value, digits is digits
        Some with plain
            give Some with value is (groupedText with value is plain, separator is separator)
        None
            give None

# The same with `symbol` in front of the amount and **inside** the sign,
# so a negative is `-$1,234.50` and not `$-1,234.50`. O(d²).
#
# A currency's number of minor units is `digits`, which is how a caller
# says that yen have none, rather than a table of currency codes this
# library would have to carry and keep current. A symbol that follows the
# amount is one `+` at the call site, which is why there is no argument
# here for the side.
function moneyText with value, digits, separator, symbol
    when numberText with value is value, digits is digits, separator is separator
        Some with plain
            if startsWith with value is plain, prefix is "-"
                give Some with value is ("-" + symbol + (dropFirst with value is plain, count is 1))
            give Some with value is (symbol + plain)
        None
            give None

# A rendered number with `separator` between each group of three digits
# to the left of the point. O(d²).
#
# The sign comes off first and the function calls itself on what is left,
# so the grouping never sees one. The fraction is never grouped, however
# long it is, which is what every convention that groups at all does.
#
# The decimal point stays the language's own. A locale that writes it as
# a comma is one `replace` at the call site, and the library does not
# guess which one a program wants.
function groupedText with value, separator
    if startsWith with value is value, prefix is "-"
        give "-" + (groupedText with value is (dropFirst with value is value, count is 1), separator is separator)
    if value contains "."
        give (groupedDigits with digits is (before with value is value, delimiter is "."), separator is separator) + "." + (after with value is value, delimiter is ".")
    give groupedDigits with digits is value, separator is separator

# A run of digits with `separator` after the first group and then every
# three. O(d²).
#
# `lead` is a local binding rather than the same call written twice: the
# first group's length is both where the answer starts and where the walk
# picks up, and computing it once is the difference between naming a value
# and asserting that two expressions agree.
function groupedDigits with digits, separator
    with lead is (leadingGroup of digits)
    give groupFrom with digits is digits, separator is separator, index is lead, taken is (slice with value is digits, start is 0, stop is lead)

function groupFrom with digits, separator, index, taken
    if index < (textLength of digits)
        give groupFrom with digits is digits, separator is separator, index is index + 3, taken is taken + separator + (slice with value is digits, start is index, stop is index + 3)
    give taken

# How many digits are in the first group: what is left over after taking
# threes off the end, and a full three when nothing is left over.
#
# This is the boundary a naive grouping gets wrong. Without the second
# case `"123456"` begins with a separator, because six divides by three
# and the leading group would be empty.
function leadingGroup of digits
    when mod with value is (textLength of digits), divisor is 3
        Some with remainder
            if remainder is 0
                give 3
            give remainder
        None
            give 3

# --- bitwise, on the low 32 bits, unsigned ------------------------------

foreign bitAnd is anywhere
    from "zd:number" as "bitAnd"
    takes left is Whole, right is Whole
    gives pure Whole

foreign bitOr is anywhere
    from "zd:number" as "bitOr"
    takes left is Whole, right is Whole
    gives pure Whole

foreign bitXor is anywhere
    from "zd:number" as "bitXor"
    takes left is Whole, right is Whole
    gives pure Whole

foreign shiftLeft is anywhere
    from "zd:number" as "shiftLeft"
    takes value is Whole, places is Whole
    gives pure Whole

foreign shiftRight is anywhere
    from "zd:number" as "shiftRight"
    takes value is Whole, places is Whole
    gives pure Whole

# `Math.imul`: the product of two 32-bit numbers, wrapped back into 32
# bits. `*` cannot do this — `a * b` on two numbers near 2^32 is exact in
# f64 up to 2^53 and silently loses low bits above it, and the low bits are
# the whole point of a mixing function.
foreign wrappingProduct is anywhere
    from "zd:number" as "wrappingProduct"
    takes left is Whole, right is Whole
    gives pure Whole

# The unsigned 32-bit window of any `Whole`, which is also how a sum is
# wrapped back into the window: `toUnsigned32 of (a + b)`. O(1).
function toUnsigned32 of value
    give shiftRight with value is value, places is 0

# --- a seeded generator, written in ZDeceptron -------------------------
#
# mulberry32. A seed is an ordinary `Whole`; a program threads it through
# its own state, which is what makes a game engine the pure
# `(state, input, seed) → state` function §17.4.9's technique already
# writes. Every function here is O(1) and none of them reads anything.
#
# These were written when a parameter was the only way to name an
# intermediate, and `mixA` naming its argument once and reading it four
# times is that shape. It is no longer the only way: `with name is value`
# inside a body binds a local, and `groupedDigits` below uses one. These
# three are left as they are because each is also a step of mulberry32
# worth naming on its own, not because the language forces it.

# The next seed in the sequence. O(1).
function nextSeed of seed
    give toUnsigned32 of (seed + 1831565813)

function mixA of a
    give wrappingProduct with left is (bitXor with left is a, right is (shiftRight with value is a, places is 15)), right is (bitOr with left is a, right is 1)

function mixB of t
    give bitXor with left is (toUnsigned32 of (t + (wrappingProduct with left is (bitXor with left is t, right is (shiftRight with value is t, places is 7)), right is (bitOr with left is t, right is 61)))), right is t

function mixC of t
    give bitXor with left is t, right is (shiftRight with value is t, places is 14)

# The generator's output for a seed: a `Whole` in `0 … 4294967295`. O(1).
#
# Pure in `seed`, so the same seed always gives the same number, on every
# machine and in every placement including `static`.
function randomBits of seed
    give mixC of (mixB of (mixA of seed))

# A `Whole` in `0 … bound - 1`, or `None` when `bound` is 0. O(1).
#
# `mod` of a 32-bit value, so the distribution is uniform to within one
# part in 2^32 divided by `bound` — negligible for a board size and worth
# saying rather than implying.
#
# The `Option` is `mod`'s and is not decorated here: a range with nothing
# in it has no member to draw, and a fallback chosen by the library would
# be a number the caller did not ask for. `valueOr` is the caller's.
function randomBelow with seed, bound
    give mod with value is (randomBits of seed), divisor is bound

# A `Decimal` in `0 … 1`, excluding 1. O(1).
function randomDecimal of seed
    give (decimalOf of (randomBits of seed)) / 4294967296
